Timeline

  1. Read the review on #27 before touching anything. Five of its six findings
     are about a claim being stronger than its evidence, which is the same
     defect the change itself is about, one level up.

  2. Rebased onto origin/main FIRST, before any new work, because two of the
     conflicts delete live guards and resolving them afterwards would have
     meant re-testing everything. Makefile: kept main's observation-selftest
     AND added marker-selftest. change/activate.sh: took main's side whole
     (git checkout --ours), then re-added only the marker call, addressed
     through ./change/marker.sh because main's cd already lands at the repo
     root. The $HERE workaround and the "found, not fixed" claim about
     activate.sh are gone -- both were written against a tree 58 commits old.

  3. FAILED FIRST, and this is the one worth keeping. The mutation harness's
     `dev-label-reaches-the-forge` mutation SURVIVED: zero cases failed. The
     health-test case meant to cover it ran health.sh without --pr, so the
     label block never executed and its `!dev-4:healthy` assertion was
     satisfied by output that could not have contained the string under any
     circumstances. A passing test asserting nothing, in the change that
     argues against exactly that, found only because the mutation was run.
     Fixed with --pr against a shimmed gh; second run killed it, 1 case.

  4. Second failure, smaller: the first `mixed.tsv` fixture held one matched
     and one unobserved sample and was asserted to derive `diverged`. It
     derives `unreachable`, correctly -- nothing in it positively saw another
     build. The fixture was wrong, not the precedence rule. Caught by the
     suite on its first run.

  5. shellcheck found two real things in the new code: SC2125 on an unquoted
     `?x=1` in a test env-var assignment (a glob, not a query string, to the
     shell) and SC1007 on `VAR= \` before a line continuation.

  6. NOT OBSERVED, marked [H]: the live beacon GET. It fires only after a
     marker is delivered to a reachable sink, and the brief forbids a real
     request to the example host, so URL construction, validation and encoding
     are tested offline and the curl line itself is reviewed, not run.
     Also not observed: deploy.started from activate.sh, which exits 6 on this
     host before reaching it; and any real staging or production estate.

